  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 125 AM Sunday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... Seasonable temperatures, dry weather and abundant \nhigh clouds the rest of this weekend.\n\nS and SW flow ahead of a mid-level short wave trough currently over \nthe central plains will spread abundant mid and high clouds across \nour area through the rest of the weekend.  But it will remain dry \ngiven the stable low levels and relative minimum in pwat and low \nlevel dwpts.  The current airmass will support highs within a few \ndeg of seasonal averages.  \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... Chance of showers from late Wednesday night through \nFriday night or Saturday. Continued very warm from mid through late \nweek. A bit cooler (but still above normal temperatures).\n\nThere is a chance of showers starting Wednesday night in the west, \nthen progressing NE over our region Thursday and Thursday night. \nHowever, confidence remains low given the spread in the models and \nthe seemingly endless \"kicking the can\" down the road on days 5-7. \nEven a week ago, models (we) were advertising increasing shower \nchances on days 5-7. Yet, it has yet to verify. This next chance of \nshowers is advertised as a large spoke of energy tracks NE across \nthere region during Thursday with the main upper trough well to our \nNW over the Midwest at that time. Thus, this forecast will likely \ncontinue to be refined over time as models further adjust on the \ntiming and strength of the energy this far SE at that time. \nRegardless, the SE upper ridge shifts just offshore. The south to \nsouthwest flow ahead of the trough upstream will keep temperatures a \ngood 10-15 degrees (if not more) above normal Wednesday through \nFriday. Lows around 60 and highs in the mid to upper 70s is more \ntypical of early September. \n\nThen, all bets are off for the weekend as the models give and take. \nRight now it appears that the main dynamics of the next upper trough \nwill again pass to our west and north this weekend, meaning low \nchances of showers and continued warmth, but only 5-8 degrees above \nnormal instead of 15.",
